#{[17592186360545 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}(x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}(x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{-2}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{-2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{-2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{-3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-2{t}^{-3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-2({t}^{2})({t}^{-3})+2{e}^{-t}({e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360547 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}t+\\operatorname{cos}t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360548 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t-4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)+\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t+4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\operatorname{sin}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)+2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}2t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360550 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xz}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{xy}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t-1},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t-1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t-1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t-1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t-1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t-1}}{{e}^{t-1}}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t+1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360556 "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}2s}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}2s)+\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}2s}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}2s}\\right)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2s}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}2s \\\\  & =-2{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360577 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $y=t+1$ and $z=t-1$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =[(t+1)^{2}-(t-1)^{2}]\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot (t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot (t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot 1-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\  & =4+4\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360578 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y={e}^{t},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360579 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}(xz)+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}(xz)+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}(xz)+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,y={t}^{2},$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t}+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362333 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$"] [17592186362486 "Recall that the circle\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1\n$$\n\nin the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9}+\\frac{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }},\\hspace{0.167em}3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.167em}z⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $(\\theta ,z)\\in C$ and $C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$"] [17592186362489 "We found that a parametrization of the cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,z⟩\\hspace{2em}(\\theta ,z)\\in C.\n$$\n\nRestricting the parametrization of the cylinder to $z=2,$ we get a parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )$ for the heating element:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )=\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,2)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,2⟩\n$$\n\nFor convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )$ in column vector column:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )=\\begin{bmatrix}x(\\theta ) \\\\ y(\\theta ) \\\\ z(\\theta )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nNotice that this curve is traversed *counterclockwise* when viewed from above.\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing unit normal vector to this curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta ).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta )=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) \\\\ {y}^{'}(\\theta ) \\\\ {z}^{'}(\\theta )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Since $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta )$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians clockwise $(ϕ=-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nUsing the hint, the rotation matrix corresponding to a $ϕ=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ counterclockwise rotation about the $z$-axis is\n\n$$\n{R}_{z}(\\theta )=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta )$ by $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we normalize the vector found in Step 2:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}|| & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}}{||\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x(\\theta ) \\\\ y(\\theta ) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362496 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $E$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $E$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{E}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit vector to $E.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd5e4c7ab86cdb999e239e986713a51c07b8decb629b1ee4a656077590f04bb.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{E}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{E}(6y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}2xy+\\frac{1}{3}xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}\\frac{7}{3}xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362505 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/46/4693916207540a6453c6c3775824ba577e62fea0a18b07e96f8814901d9ad80e.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(-2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y-(-4y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362524 "If $\\mathbf{G}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{i}}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{j}}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{G}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{G}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{G}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{G}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{G} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-xy-2{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362533 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+(-3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362536 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0-(3x\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362539 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={∬}_{D}\\underbrace{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }\\mathbf{F}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}\\underbrace{(3x{y}^{2})}_{P}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+\\underbrace{(-{y}^{3})}_{Q}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$"] [17592186362542 "Notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-6(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362552 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362555 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363031 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186364309 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={x}^{2}y \\\\ {x}^{2}y-x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-x-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,2)={f}_{y}(1,2)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,2)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-1)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-1)j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy-1)j \\\\ t(1,2) & =(1)^{2}\\,i-(2(1)(2)-1)j \\\\  & =i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186364316 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y \\\\ 2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-3{x}^{2}+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -2(y-3x) & =0 \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ 2x(3x)-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-3$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-3).$"] [17592186365398 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}z}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}z}.$\n\nHere the free parameter is $z$. The stated direct-coordinate convention therefore requires the third component to be $z$."] [17592186365405 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}3{y}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186365412 "Recall that the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =49{sec}^{2}⁡t-49{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =49({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,t) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}7\\operatorname{sec}t},\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{tan}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}7\\operatorname{sec}t}.$"] [17592186365419 "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}\\operatorname{sec}t},y,\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{tan}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}\\operatorname{sec}t}.$"] [17592186365426 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"] [17592186365433 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"] [17592186365440 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+25{z}^{2}=100\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"] [17592186365555 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-3)(-1)-(2)(2))\\mathbf{i}-((1)(-1)-(2)(4))\\mathbf{j}+((1)(2)-(-3)(4))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-4)\\mathbf{i}-(-1-8)\\mathbf{j}+(2+12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-1\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+14\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366191 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(5\\cdot 0-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(5\\cdot 1-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\mathbf{i}-0\\cdot \\mathbf{j}+(5+\\sqrt{2})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,5+\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366198 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ x & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-2+2)\\mathbf{i}-(-4+x)\\mathbf{j}+(8-2x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,4-x,8-2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(4-x)^{2}+(8-2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-8x+{x}^{2}+64-32x+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-40x+80} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+80 & =20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-40x+60 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2,\\,6.$"] [17592186367659 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}r{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367666 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/36/36839d1fbde65e86df7335d40374fe72a5ff43ed92b89405327406a62dc8763c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367673 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can 