 .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367680 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/7d/7d3ef0b0a9a2f1c9ab60b4ed09f8a0318c57f74236e404baf181e0197ef66002.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+2=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}rz({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{