a +3)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,-8\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{-8}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +2}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367729 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =3 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le z\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}^{6}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369258 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369265 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =⟨(-2+t(-1-(-2))),\\hspace{0.222em}(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =3(t-2)+2(2-t) \\\\  & =3t-6+4-2t \\\\  & =t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\cdot (-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369272 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369291 "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $1,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369298 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$\\mathbf{F}$ is **not** conservative” is false. The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\ne 0\n$$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186369305 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [17592186369312 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$”."] [17592186369399 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369406 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369413 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369420 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}6\\operatorname{cos}2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369427 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=-4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+9{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=-\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{t}^{3}\\cdot (-4t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =12[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369448 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+4{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=-\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369462 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/a8/a8e93055fb9a460ab7757475c9c381c191204eae72231921da870b8d727bf988.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =9+(-4)+(-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369469 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/52/527eb8572b90c3d2a4449b69015a9b94f4d121b094be6629e1b92c45f2ff0eb0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =1+7+(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369476 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/be/be04b9b505d71a99f7333ca9e9e9755c78453421f5aaf197306cbc5f2ca8df63.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{3}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =7+(-9)-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369483 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/85/85a8ea287d2cb8e3b70e332ac444a9e06262fef35369d1bad1f24ea5c9d31687.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer and inner curves are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-6-2-(-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369490 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-5+(-2)-(-9) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369504 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{4}y-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot ({3}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{728\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369511 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\operatorname{ln}({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2\\le r\\le 4,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({4}^{4}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-120\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369518 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{3}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${\\mathrm{Δ}}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${\\mathrm{Δ}}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186369873 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=3.\n$$"] [17592186370913 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{6{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371665 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $y$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186371721 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371749 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{y}+z{e}^{x})\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+{e}^{z})\\,j+(y{e}^{z}+{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,1) & =({e}^{0}+(1){e}^{0})i+((0){e}^{0}+{e}^{1})j+((0){e}^{1}+{e}^{0})k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+e\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1).$"] [17592186371872 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & xy=3\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/baa8e8ab6cff8edec84d3f0d0950931f247153523e3320abbdf673161afb44ae.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371879 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-y=1,\\,3x-y=3,\\,x-3y=0,\\,x-3y=2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-y,\\,v=x-3y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitut